<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ramadanids</h1><p class="st-meta">1352 CE – 1608 CE · capital Adana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkmen dynasty that ruled Adana and the Cilician plain for over 250 years, first under the Mamluks and then under the Ottomans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluks</a> broke the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> of the Uc Ok group settled the Cilician plain. Around 1352 their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan-bey">Ramadan Bey</a> was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> as emir of the region, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> as his seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ramadanids ruled as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and grain lands of the Cilician plain, guarding the frontier with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karamanids">Karamanids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In 1516-1517 they went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> and were kept as hereditary governors of the Ottoman sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piri-mehmed-pasha-ramadanid">Piri Mehmed Pasha</a>, a 16th-century ruler, built the Great Mosque complex of Adana.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The family lost its hereditary governorship in 1608, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> became an ordinary Ottoman province. The Ramazanoglu mansion and mosque in Adana survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ramadanids.jpg" alt="Ramadanids" width="320" height="235"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuregir.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1352 CE – 1608 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Adana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ramadan Bey, Piri Mehmed Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ramazanoğulları, Ramadanid Beylik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1480&amp;focus=ramadanids" aria-label="Ramadanids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5566 845 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ramadanids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5886 1101L5815 1335M5886 1101L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5815 1335h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5886 1101h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5886 1101h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Adana. <a href="/atlas?year=1480&amp;focus=ramadanids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
